course: everyday
lesson: 119
title: Bedroom, living room and manners at home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The rooms where we rest and relax still have hidden dangers: light bulbs get hot, decorations burn, heavy lids slam and loose batteries tempt toddlers.
explain: Most of these dangers are prevented by keeping things apart: lamps away from curtains, decorations away from candles and fires, toddlers away from pets and remote controls.
explain: A home is also a place where people live together, so good manners matter there as much as anywhere: they show care and respect for the people you live with.
explain: For example, a boy in Auckland uses the last of the milk and writes «milk» on the shopping list, so nobody finds an empty carton in the morning.
fact: Light bulbs get hot, so lamps and nightlights are kept away from curtains and other fabric.
fact: Eating in bed leaves crumbs that attract insects and make the bed dirty, so food is eaten at the table.
fact: A heavy toy box lid can slam on heads and fingers, so a slow-closing lid or no lid is safer.
fact: Christmas decorations are kept away from fireplaces and candles because they catch fire.
fact: A pet can snap if a toddler pulls it, so a dog and a toddler together are watched.
fact: A remote control with a missing battery cover is a danger to toddlers, who can pull out and swallow the batteries.
fact: A guest feels welcome when you greet them, offer a drink and show them where things are.
fact: If you use the last of something, tell someone or write it on the shopping list.
fact: Borrowing something from a family member means asking first and giving it back.
fact: Saying goodnight to your family shows that you care.
fact: When grown-ups say «not in front of the kids», they mean the talk is private grown-up business, and you can ask a parent later if you are worried.
exercise: Your nightlight is touching the curtain. What should you do? | Move it away from the fabric | Leave it, nightlights are always cold | Wrap the curtain around it to dim it | Turn it round to face the curtain
exercise: Why should you not eat crisps in bed? | Crumbs attract insects and make the bed dirty | Crisps taste worse and go soggy when you eat them lying down | It makes you sleep too long | The bed gets too warm
exercise: Your little cousin plays with a toy box with a heavy lid. What is safer? | A box with a slow-closing lid or no lid | A box with an even heavier lid | A box that locks from the inside so nobody can get in | A box with a lid that snaps shut
exercise: Where should paper Christmas decorations be hung? | Away from the fireplace and candles | Right above the open fire | Around the candles on the table, where everyone can see them | Inside the lampshade
exercise: Your toddler brother is pulling the dog's ears on the sofa. What should happen? | Keep an eye on them and stop him, because the dog may snap | Let him, dogs never mind | Join in to make it a game | Leave them alone together on the sofa so they can make friends
exercise: The battery cover of the TV remote is missing. What do you do? | Tell a grown-up and keep the remote away from the toddler | Take the batteries out and give them to the toddler to hold instead | Leave it on the floor | Push the batteries in harder
exercise: A guest comes to your home. What is polite? | Greet them, offer a drink and show them where things are | Keep playing your game and say nothing until they talk to you | Ask them how long they are staying | Go to your room until they leave
exercise: You pour the last of the milk. What should you do? | Tell someone or write it on the shopping list | Put the empty carton back in the fridge | Hide the carton in the bin | Say nothing and hope someone notices the next time they look
exercise: You want to borrow your sister's headphones. What is right? | Ask first and give them back | Take them quietly while she is out | Borrow them and keep them for good | Take them and say they were lost
exercise: You are tired and go to bed while your family watches TV. What is kind? | Say goodnight to your family | Go up to bed without a single word | Just turn the TV off on your way | Shout goodnight from upstairs at midnight
exercise: Why should a lit candle never stand near the Christmas tree? | The decorations and the tree can catch fire | The candle makes the tree grow | The warm tree makes the candle melt and go out much too fast | The candle melts the presents
exercise: Why should you give back what you borrowed from your dad? | It is his, and giving it back shows respect | He will forget about it anyway | Borrowed things belong to you after a day | Only expensive things need to go back
test: Why might a lamp start a fire if pushed against a curtain? | The hot bulb can heat the fabric until it burns | Curtains make electricity | Lamps give off sparks whenever their shade is touched by cloth | Curtains make the bulb explode
test: A girl in Texas finds ants in her bed. What most likely brought them? | Crumbs from eating in bed | An open window left open all winter | A clean pillowcase on her pillow | Her school books on the shelf
test: Why are toy boxes with heavy lids a danger to small children? | The lid can slam down on heads and fingers | Small children cannot open any box with a lid on their own | Heavy lids make toys break | Children get bored of heavy boxes
test: Why must a toddler never be given a remote with loose batteries? | He can pull the batteries out and swallow them | He will keep changing the channel and break the TV's screen | The remote will stop working forever | Batteries make toddlers too excited
test: Which is true about a calm family dog and a toddler? | Even a calm dog can snap if it is pulled or hurt | A calm dog never snaps at anyone | Dogs only snap at grown-ups | Toddlers cannot hurt dogs
test: Your aunt visits for the first time. Which is most welcoming? | Saying hello, offering her a drink and showing her the bathroom | Showing her your new game and then ignoring her for the whole visit | Asking what present she brought | Leaving the room when she arrives
test: Why does writing «milk» on the shopping list help the family? | Whoever shops knows to buy more | It makes the milk last much longer | It is an old rule of every fridge | It is only for grown-ups to read
test: Why is it wrong to take your brother's bike without asking? | It belongs to him, and asking shows respect | Bikes break if a second person rides them | He might want to sell it | It is only wrong if you scratch it
test: can i have my crisps in bed tonight | Better at the table, crumbs bring bugs | Yes, crumbs just disappear by the morning if you sleep on them | Yes, just shake the sheet | Only if the light is off
test: used the last of the milk oops | Tell someone or add it to the shopping list | Put the empty carton back in | Say nothing, they'll find out | Fill it with water
test: borrowed my sisters charger, she didnt notice lol | Ask first next time and give it back | Keep it, she has another | Fine if she never asks | Hide it in your room
test: nightlight's stuck right up against the curtain | Move it away, bulbs get hot and can start a fire | Fine, nightlights never get warm | Fine if the curtain is thin | Only big lamps get hot enough to cause any problem with curtains
explain: A boy in Ohio fell asleep with his phone charging under his pillow; it overheated and scorched the sheet, and his mum smelt the burning just in time.
explain: A toddler in Leeds pulled a TV off a low stand by climbing on it; it fell on his leg and broke it.
fact: Phones and tablets charging under pillows or blankets can overheat and start a fire.
fact: TVs that are not fixed to the wall or a stand can tip over onto small children.
test: phone charging under my pillow all night | Charge it on a hard surface, it can overheat and burn | It's fine, a soft pillow keeps the phone safe and stops it falling | Put it under the duvet too | Only if it's a new phone
test: baby bro climbing the telly stand | Get him down and tell an adult, the TV can fall on him | Let him, he's having fun | Hold the TV steady with both hands while he climbs up to the top | Put him on top of the TV
test: blind cord hangin' by the baby's cot | Tell an adult, cords can wrap round a baby's neck | Leave it, babies like it | Tie it to the cot | Make it longer
test: did u hear a boy's phone under his pillow "caught fire n burnt his whole room down". what's probs exaggerated | The whole room; a phone under a pillow can scorch and start a fire | That phones get hot when they charge | That pillows and blankets trap heat | That you should always charge a phone on a hard surface, not in bed
test: my aunt says her friend's toddler climbed the telly n it fell on him. real lesson? | Fix TVs to the wall or stand so they cannot tip | Toddlers only climb TVs that are switched on and showing cartoons | Big TVs are too heavy to ever fall over | Put a toy on top so he will not climb
test: everyone's saying Ollie's house is "full of cockroaches" cos he eats in bed. spread it? | No, it is unkind and probably not true | Yes, people should know before they visit | Yes, but only post it in the group chat | Yes, if you say you heard it from someone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which lamps and nightlights are to be kept away from curtains and fabric. | Light bulbs grow hot and may set fabric alight | Fabric reduces the brightness of the bulb | Curtains interfere with the lamp's electricity | Light causes the curtains to shrink in size
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger posed by a remote control lacking its battery cover? | A toddler may remove and swallow the batteries | The remote may switch the television on alone | The remote will cease to function within a day | The batteries will lose their charge very fast
exam: Whereas a member of the household has used the last of the milk, state the conduct consistent with good manners. | Tell someone or write it on the shopping list | Put the empty carton back into the fridge | Say nothing and hope that someone notices | Blame another member of the household
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a dog and a toddler in the same room. | They are watched, as a pulled pet may snap | They are safe together, as dogs love children | They are left alone so that they make friends | They are kept together to teach the dog patience
test: grown ups went quiet n said "not in front of the kids" when i came in the living room | It is private grown-up talk; give them space, and ask a parent later if worried | It means you did something wrong | It means you must listen quietly at the door to find out the secret | It means there is a surprise present for you hidden somewhere, so go and search for it
test: my big bro shut his door n yelled "i'm on the phone to my girlfriend, get out" | He wants privacy to talk to someone he likes; leave him be, do not listen in | Listen in and tell mum everything | Shout his girlfriend's name out loud outside his door so that she hears you tease him | Take his phone away
test: heard mum n dad arguing in the bedroom bout "who she was texting" | It is not your fault; do not spread it, and tell a trusted adult if you are worried | Go in and pick a side, because they need you to tell them who is right | Tell everyone at school | Read the texts on mum's phone yourself to find out who she was texting and tell dad
